id: pm-xv92s7
title: "Windows recovery commands lose byte-exactness through npm's pm.cmd launcher: its %* forwarding parses an argument holding a double quote and a cmd metacharacter a second time"
description: "Found 2026-11-24 by the CodeRabbit review of PR #1779 (pm-gh1774, pm-kjoudp). The cmd dialect makes a pasted recovery command safe for the line cmd.exe parses: an argument holding % or a double quote is caret-escaped, quotes included. On a normal Windows npm install, pm resolves to npm's generated pm.cmd, which forwards %* into its own batch line. That second parse sees the CommandLineToArgvW spelling again, so for an argument such as a\"b & calc the embedded quote flips cmd.exe's quote state and the & splits the shim's line. Main renders the same argument unescaped and splits already at the first parse, so this is not a regression, but the guarantee is incomplete for the most common Windows install. Options to decide: render a double-parse-safe spelling (^^^ before each metacharacter) when pm resolves to a batch launcher; a declared cmd-batch dialect; or an argv-preserving recovery form (a JSON argv receipt that the SDK replays without a shell). Duplicate check: searched npm cmd shim, second parse, percent star, batch launcher; pm-kjoudp and pm-gh1774 (closed) cover the first parse only."
type: Issue
status: open
priority: 2
tags[4]: agent-ux,recovery,security,windows
